Lab 12: Configuring Advanced Neutron Networking
Objectives
Configure VPN as a Service (VPNaaS) in OpenStack Neutron
Implement VXLAN and GRE tunneling for network isolation
Configure security groups and DHCP for tenant networks
Understand advanced Neutron networking concepts in OpenStack
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment with Neutron installed (Queens or later)
Administrative access to the OpenStack CLI
Basic understanding of OpenStack networking concepts
Installed packages: python-neutronclient, openvpn
Two tenant networks pre-configured for testing
Task 1: Set Up VPN as a Service (VPNaaS)
1.1 Install VPNaaS Components
# On the controller node
sudo apt-get install neutron-vpnaas neutron-l3-agent
sudo service neutron-server restart
Explanation:
VPNaaS requires the Neutron VPN agent and L3 agent for routing. The package provides IPSec VPN functionality.

1.2 Configure VPN Service
# Enable VPNaaS in neutron.conf
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/neutron.conf service_providers service_provider VPN:openswan:neutron.services.vpn.service_drivers.ipsec.IPsecVPNDriver:default

# Restart services
sudo systemctl restart neutron-server
Expected Outcome:
VPN service is now enabled in Neutron. Verify with:

openstack network service provider list | grep VPN
1.3 Create VPN Configuration
# Create IKE policy
openstack vpn ike policy create ikepolicy1 \
    --auth-algorithm sha1 \
    --encryption-algorithm aes-256 \
    --phase1-negotiation-mode main \
    --ike-version v1 \
    --pfs group5 \
    --lifetime units=seconds,value=3600

# Create IPSec policy
openstack vpn ipsec policy create ipsecpolicy1 \
    --auth-algorithm sha1 \
    --encryption-algorithm aes-256 \
    --encapsulation-mode tunnel \
    --transform-protocol esp \
    --pfs group5 \
    --lifetime units=seconds,value=3600
Troubleshooting Tip:
If policies fail, check /var/log/neutron/neutron-vpn-agent.log for errors.

Task 2: Implement VXLAN and GRE for Network Isolation
2.1 Configure VXLAN
# Edit neutron.conf
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/plugins/ml2/ml2_conf.ini ml2 type_drivers "local,flat,vlan,gre,vxlan"
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/plugins/ml2/ml2_conf.ini ml2 tenant_network_types vxlan
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/plugins/ml2/ml2_conf.ini vxlan vni_ranges 1000:2000

# Restart services
sudo systemctl restart neutron-server
Key Concept:
VXLAN provides Layer 2 isolation using 24-bit VNI identifiers, enabling larger scale networks.

2.2 Create VXLAN Network
openstack network create vxlan-net --provider-network-type vxlan
openstack subnet create vxlan-subnet --network vxlan-net --subnet-range 192.168.100.0/24
2.3 Configure GRE (Alternative to VXLAN)
# Edit ml2_conf.ini
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/plugins/ml2/ml2_conf.ini ml2 tenant_network_types gre
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/plugins/ml2/ml2_conf.ini gre tunnel_id_ranges 1:1000

# Restart services
sudo systemctl restart neutron-server
Comparison:
GRE uses simpler encapsulation but lacks VXLAN's scalability features.

Task 3: Configure Security Groups and DHCP
3.1 Create Custom Security Group
openstack security group create web-access --description "Allow HTTP/HTTPS"
openstack security group rule create --protocol tcp --dst-port 80:80 web-access
openstack security group rule create --protocol tcp --dst-port 443:443 web-access
3.2 Configure DHCP Agent
# Edit dhcp_agent.ini
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/dhcp_agent.ini DEFAULT interface_driver linuxbridge
sudo crudini --set /etc/neutron/dhcp_agent.ini DEFAULT enable_isolated_metadata true

# Restart agent
sudo systemctl restart neutron-dhcp-agent
3.3 Verify DHCP Functionality
# Create test instance
openstack server create --image cirros --flavor m1.tiny --network vxlan-net test-vm

# Check DHCP lease
neutron port-list --device-id $(openstack server show test-vm -c id -f value)
Expected Outcome:
The instance should receive an IP from the 192.168.100.0/24 range.

